# VISION — Product vision

> **Purpose:** the product's "why" — who it's for, what problem it solves, what it
> deliberately does NOT do. One page, stable for the product's lifetime.
> Written early (phase 1), revisited only on strategic change.

> **RU:** видение продукта — «зачем»: для кого, какую проблему решаем, чего
> сознательно НЕ делаем. Одна страница, живёт весь жизненный цикл.

## Vision statement
<!-- 1–2 sentences: the product in its final form. Example:
     "A documentation system that lets one agent order a whole repo's docs
      from an idea — nothing forgotten, everything linked." -->

## Problem statement
<!-- 2–4 sentences: who suffers, what pain, what happens today without the product.
     RU: чья боль, что происходит сейчас без продукта. -->

## Target audience
<!-- Primary + secondary segments. For each: who, context, main need. -->

## Value proposition / differentiation
<!-- Why this product wins: unique capability, price, simplicity, speed, ecosystem.
     vs: what alternatives exist and what they lack. -->

## Product principles
<!-- 3–5 durable principles that guide every feature decision.
     Example: "The map comes last", "Machine truth before code". -->

## Scope
| In scope | Out of scope (explicitly) |
|----------|---------------------------|
| `...`    | `...`                     |

> Out-of-scope list is as important as features — it prevents requirement creep.
> **RU:** список «не делаем» так же важен, как функции — он защищает от расползания требований.

## Long-term success criteria
<!-- The tests that prove the vision was right, measured in quarters/years:
     adoption, retention, revenue, time-to-value. -->

---
> **Rule:** if the vision paragraph is unclear — the project is not ready for
> requirement docs. Clarify the vision first, then write PRD.
> **RU:** не ясно видение → не пишем дальше; сначала уточняем, потом PRD.